# Test: ze doctor warns that BGP cannot bind on an LCP-shadowed interface when
#       vpp.lcp.netns names a namespace ze does not run in.
# VALIDATES: fixit-vpp-lcp-reachability AC-12 -- the DELIVERED checkVPPLCPNetns
# (internal/plugins/iface/vpp/doctor.go) reached from the USER entry point.
# Registration, phase, platform filtering and the diagnostic code all have to
# line up for this to fire; the unit tests in
# internal/plugins/iface/vpp/register_test.go exercise the decision logic, and
# this proves an operator actually sees it. Required by
# ai/rules/repo-maintenance.md ("Doctor Checks" -> "Test Requirement": unit AND
# functional), modelled on test/ui/doctor-vpp-lcp-plugin.ci.
# PREVENTS: the warning going silent -- the netns leaf DEFAULTS to "dataplane"
# (ze-vpp-conf.yang), so the broken-by-default case is the one omitting the
# leaf entirely. A check that only fired on an explicitly-set netns would look
# correct in review and never reach the operator it exists for.
#
# No option=needs-linux: the config leg of the check (doctor.go
# lcpNetnsConfigDiagnostic) reads the config tree only and speaks on every
# platform, which is what the three assertions below gate. The check DOES have a
# platform gate and DOES open a probe -- lcpNetnsDir is /var/run/netns/ on Linux
# and empty elsewhere, so the host leg adds a second diagnostic there when the
# namespace is absent. Nothing here asserts on that leg, so the file passes on
# both, and the unit tests in internal/plugins/iface/vpp/register_test.go drive
# the host leg with a temporary directory instead.
#
# The netns leaf is deliberately OMITTED: "dataplane" is the YANG default and
# doctor.go re-applies it, so this covers the default-configuration path.
#
# This file runs exactly ONE command on purpose. expect=stdout:contains= is
# matched against the file's ACCUMULATED stdout, not per command, so a second
# `ze explain doctor-vpp-lcp-netns` in the same file prints the code name and
# satisfies the code assertion on its own -- with checkVPPLCPNetns stubbed out
# the first draft of this test still passed that assertion. Keeping the file to
# one command is what makes all three assertions gate.

tmpfs=vpp-lcp-netns.conf:terminator=EOF_CONF
bgp {
    router-id 1.2.3.4
    session {
        asn {
            local 65000
        }
    }
}

vpp {
    lcp {
        enabled true
    }
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=ze doctor --json vpp-lcp-netns.conf
expect=stdout:contains=doctor-vpp-lcp-netns
expect=stdout:contains=is not root-reachable
expect=stdout:contains=dataplane
# NOTE: an earlier DRAFT of this file, never committed, also ran `ze explain
#   doctor-vpp-lcp-netns` and asserted on it. Both were dropped before the file
#   landed, because they made it WEAKER, not
#   stronger. Assertions match accumulated stdout, so explain's own output
#   printed the code name and satisfied `contains=doctor-vpp-lcp-netns` even
#   with checkVPPLCPNetns stubbed to return nil -- proven by a mutation run.
#   What the dropped assertion actually proved is still gated elsewhere:
#   TestRegistryKnownCodes (internal/core/diagnostic/registry_test.go:12)
#   walks EVERY registered code and requires a non-empty title and
#   description, and test/ui/command-contract-explain.ci gates the `ze explain`
#   CLI contract itself. The three remaining assertions now all gate on the
#   doctor output this test exists to prove.
